      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14194326/assets/40faa5a8b9bc08fcef23477a938c24d9aa6ebbc987b8eaeb3175cdec47bc00b3.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=4.$ Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4)-g(4-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(4)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
